
📌 Version: Switch expressions → Java 12 (preview) → Java 14 (standard). Java 17 LTS mein common.
(Pattern matching for switch → Java 17 preview → Java 21 standard — alag topic, pattern matching file dekho.)


🔹 Before (Traditional switch STATEMENT)

String day = "MONDAY";
int num;

switch (day) {
    case "MONDAY":
        num = 1;
        break;
    case "TUESDAY":
        num = 2;
        break;
    default:
        num = 0;
}

❌ Problems with old style
- Required break (forget → fall-through bugs)
- More boilerplate
- Not an expression (can’t assign / return directly)
- Less readable


🔹 After — Switch EXPRESSION (arrow form)

int num = switch (day) {
    case "MONDAY"  -> 1;
    case "TUESDAY" -> 2;
    default        -> 0;
};

✅ Returns a value → assign / return kar sakte ho
✅ Arrow `->` = NO fall-through (break ki zaroorat nahi)
✅ Cleaner


🔹 Statement vs Expression (interview diff)

|                    | Switch STATEMENT           | Switch EXPRESSION              |
|--------------------|----------------------------|--------------------------------|
| Returns value?     | No                         | Yes                            |
| Fall-through?      | Yes (colon `:` + break)    | No with `->`                   |
| Exhaustive?        | Optional default           | MUST cover all cases (or default) |
| Introduced modern  | Classic Java               | Java 14 standard               |


🔹 Multiple labels (comma)

int num = switch (day) {
    case "MONDAY", "FRIDAY", "SUNDAY" -> 1;   // weekends/week start style grouping
    case "TUESDAY"                     -> 2;
    default                            -> 0;
};


🔹 Block + yield (jab multi-line logic chahiye)

int num = switch (day) {
    case "MONDAY" -> {
        System.out.println("Start of week");
        yield 1;          // yield = return value from switch expression block
    }
    case "TUESDAY" -> 2;
    default -> 0;
};

⚡ `yield` sirf switch EXPRESSION ke block mein.
   Old `break` value return nahi karta — `yield` karta hai.


🔹 Colon form with yield (bhi valid expression)

int num = switch (day) {
    case "MONDAY":
        yield 1;
    case "TUESDAY":
        yield 2;
    default:
        yield 0;
};

Prefer `->` for clarity; colon+yield jab intentional grouping/fall-through-style structure chahiye (rare).


🔹 Exhaustiveness

Switch expression MUST produce a value for every possible input:

// enum — saari constants cover OR default
enum Day { MON, TUE, WED }
int n = switch (day) {
    case MON -> 1;
    case TUE -> 2;
    case WED -> 3;     // default optional if all enum constants covered
};

// String / int etc. → usually default REQUIRED
int n2 = switch (day) {
    case "MONDAY" -> 1;
    default -> 0;      // warna compile error
};


🔹 Quick link: Pattern Matching for switch (Java 21)
Sealed types + type patterns:

return switch (shape) {
    case Circle c    -> Math.PI * c.radius * c.radius;
    case Rectangle r -> r.length * r.width;
    case Square s    -> s.side * s.side;
};


🧠 Interview one-liner
“Switch expressions allow switch to return a value, eliminate fall-through with arrow syntax,
and require exhaustiveness — use yield when the case is a multi-line block.”
